Carafe: with X87FastStack, the x87 stack pass stays on its fast path when a block reads registers pushed by an
earlier block. Without it, the first such read writes the block's stack back to memory and moves the rest of the
block to the slow path, which recomputes register addresses from TOP and writes every value, TOP and the tag word
again at the end. Every branch and every call return starts a new block, so float code with branches, loops or
float return values paid this all the time. Now each such register is loaded once, and at the block's end only the
registers that changed are stored and only the tags that changed are written; a register pushed and popped inside
the block costs nothing. FXAM still reads the stored tag of a register it has not changed. Assumes, as FEX already
does, that the program neither overflows the x87 stack nor reads registers it has popped. Off by default.
MIT, as the files it changes.

